# Editors, each opened on the same four lines of Jabberwocky, one stanza
# per program.  Every stanza writes the demo file itself and clears the
# screen before the editor starts, so no card leans on another.

shot    ed
cap     GNU ed, the line editor, on a four-line file: print lines 1 to 4,
cap     change one word on line 3 and print the result, then Q to leave
cap     without writing.  It keeps its scratch file on /r0, the RAM disk
cap     most OS-9 systems have, so one is mounted first.
try     ed tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     mount -r=256k /r0
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     ed tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    4
send    1,4p\r
wait    3
send    3s/mimsy/MIMSY/p\r
wait    3
send    Q\r
wait    2

shot    editor
cap     A full-screen file picker in front of the umacs editor: the
cap     directory as a lettered list, + and - to page, . to change
cap     directory, a letter to open that file.  Run it bare.
try     editor
run     editor
wait    6
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    elvis
cap     Elvis, a vi with ex mode, its manual on the disk under DOC/elvis,
cap     opened on a four-line file.  Tildes mark the lines past the end.
try     elvis tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     elvis tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    \033:q!\r
wait    3

shot    elvis_input
cap     Elvis opened already in insert mode, on a new file: what you type
cap     goes straight in, and ESC gets you to the : prompt to write it.
try     elvis_input tmp/EDIT/new.txt
run     rm -f /dd/tmp/EDIT/new.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     elvis_input tmp/EDIT/new.txt
wait    8
keys    Callooh! Callay!
wait    3
snap
send    \033:q!\r
wait    3

shot    elvprsv
cap     Elvis runs this itself when it dies with a file open, to keep the
cap     buffer for elvrec.  Run by hand with nothing to preserve it says
cap     nothing, and that is all there is to see of it.
try     elvprsv
run     elvprsv
wait    5

shot    elvrec
cap     Lists the elvis buffers that were preserved by a crash, and brings
cap     one back by name.  With nothing preserved it lists nothing; asked
cap     for a file it names what it looked for.
try     elvrec
run     elvrec
wait    5
run     elvrec jabber.txt
wait    5

shot    exrecover
cap     The same job for the Berkeley vi: given a user and a file name it
cap     recovers the buffer expreserve kept when vi died.  With nothing
cap     preserved it says so.
try     exrecover tester jabber.txt
run     exrecover tester jabber.txt
wait    6

shot    em
cap     MicroEMACS 3.8b on a four-line file, with its mode line at the
cap     foot.  Control-X control-C leaves it.
try     em tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     em tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    \030\003
wait    3

shot    emacs
cap     MicroEMACS 4.00, the full one with the macro language; its macros
cap     and help are in USR/LIB/EMACS.  Control-X control-C leaves it.
try     emacs tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     emacs tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    \030\003
wait    3

shot    emacs.mm1
cap     MicroEMACS 4.00 built for the MM/1 computer -- the same editor and
cap     keys as emacs.  Control-X control-C leaves it.
try     emacs.mm1 tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     emacs.mm1 tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    \030\003
wait    3

shot    umacs
cap     uMacs 1.0, MicroEMACS in 45K -- the same Emacs keys, no macro
cap     language.  Its mode line names the file.  Control-X control-C leaves it.
try     umacs tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     umacs tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    \030\003
wait    3

shot    me
cap     MicroEMACS 3.11 with German messages -- Datei for File.
cap     Control-X control-C leaves it.
try     me tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     me tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    \030\003
wait    3

shot    mg
cap     Mg, MicroGnuEmacs: Emacs keys in 79K, its documentation under
cap     DOC/mg.  Control-X control-C leaves it.
try     mg DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     mg DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    \030\003
wait    3

shot    hexed
cap     A hex editor made out of your text editor: it writes the file out as
cap     a hex dump, opens that in the editor -e names, and when you leave it
cap     turns the dump back into the file.  Both halves are shown here doing
cap     their work -- and the editing step in between is the part this disk
cap     cannot do.
cap     hexed hands the whole thing to system() as `chd <dir> ; <editor>
cap     <file>.hd', and only Microware's own shell parses that line.  With
cap     that shell resident and SHELL naming it, vi opens on the dump;
cap     measured 2026-09-18.  So on your own OS-9 this is a working hex
cap     editor, and here it is a round trip.
cap     -t names the directory for the dump and is not optional: without it
cap     the dump goes to /r0, a RAM disk you would have to mount first
cap     (`mount -r=256k /r0').
try     hexed -t=tmp/EDIT -e=vi tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil
wait    2
run     printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     hexed -t=tmp/EDIT -e=vi tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    10

shot    hexedit
cap     HEXPERT, a hex viewer and editor, on a module from the disk: one
cap     16-byte row and the mode is its whole display.  It reads the
cap     terminal description from TERMCAP as text rather than as a file
cap     name, so the entry goes into the variable first -- that is what the
cap     leading `.' line does, and without it hexedit prints the terminal
cap     type and exits.
try     . SYS/termcap.entry; hexedit CMDS/today
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     hexedit CMDS/today
wait    12
kill

shot    pbyte
cap     Patches bytes in a file at a hex offset -- the smallest binary
cap     editor here.  Three bytes at offset 0 turn `Twas' into `ABCs'.
cap     This is the one card that shows its own setup, and deliberately:
cap     pbyte writes into the file you hand it, so a demo has to copy a
cap     sample to scratch first rather than patch something the disk
cap     ships.  Every other card hides its staging because hiding it
cap     costs the reader nothing; this one cannot.
try     cat DOC/samples/jabber.txt > tmp/pb.txt; pbyte tmp/pb.txt 0 41 42 43
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat DOC/samples/jabber.txt > tmp/pb.txt
wait    3
run     cat tmp/pb.txt
wait    3
run     pbyte tmp/pb.txt 0 41 42 43
wait    5
run     cat tmp/pb.txt
wait    3

shot    chbase
size    40 80
cap     Converts a number between bases: 255 from base 10 to 16 is FF,
cap     and a target base of 0 prints every base from 2 to 36.
try     chbase 255 10 16
run     chbase 255 10 16
wait    4
run     chbase 255 10 0
wait    6

shot    sed
cap     sed, the stream editor, substituting on every line of a file.
try     sed "s/the/THE/g" DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     sed "s/the/THE/g" DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    6

shot    sed_1.06
cap     A second build of sed, kept under its version number, doing the
cap     same substitution.
try     sed_1.06 "s/the/THE/g" DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     sed_1.06 "s/the/THE/g" DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    6

shot    view
cap     Elvis opened read-only, which is what the name `view' asks of it:
cap     the status line marks the file read-only.
try     view tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     view tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    :q!\r
wait    3

shot    vi.elvis
cap     Elvis under the name a Unix hand types.  Kept in CMDS/REBUILT
cap     because CMDS/vi is the Berkeley one.
try     vi.elvis tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     vi.elvis tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    :q!\r
wait    3

shot    vi_1.0
cap     PVIC 1.0, the Portable VI Clone, in CMDS/REBUILT because the name
cap     `vi' was taken.
try     vi_1.0 tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     vi_1.0 tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    :q!\r
wait    3

shot    vi
cap     PVIC 1.0a, the Portable VI Clone -- this disk's vi, public domain and
cap     needing no Microware module.  A whole-screen editor, so this is the
cap     screen: the whole file at once, with `~' marking every line past the
cap     end of it.  Here it has gone to the last line with `G', opened a new
cap     one with `o' and typed into it -- the change is in the buffer, not
cap     on disk.
cap     To leave: press ESC to get out of insert mode, then type `:q!' and
cap     Enter to quit without saving, or `:wq' to save and quit.
try     vi jabber.txt
# `!' is a history reference to this bash even inside quotes, and the whole
# line then dies with `Event not found' -- the poem below has two of them.
run     set +H
wait    1
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil
wait    2
run     printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r\rBeware the Jabberwock, my son!\r     The jaws that bite, the claws that catch!\rBeware the Jubjub bird, and shun\r     The frumious Bandersnatch!\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/EDIT
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     vi jabber.txt
wait    8
keys    G
wait    2
keys    o
wait    2
keys    He took his vorpal sword in hand.
wait    2
send    \e
wait    2
snap
send    :q!\r
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1

shot    ctags.elvis
cap     Builds a tags file for elvis from C source: one line per function,
cap     with the file and a search pattern that finds it.
try     ctags.elvis exrecover.c
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; rm -f /dd/tmp/EDIT/tags
run     cp /dd/SRC/effo_vi/exrecover.c /dd/tmp/EDIT/exrecover.c
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/EDIT
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     ctags.elvis exrecover.c
wait    8
run     cat tags | head -n 8
wait    4
